  // List of installed packages to exclude from type analysis. Specify the importable package name (e.g. `"PIL"` rather than `"Pillow"`). Files in ignored packages return empty content during analysis while the directory structure is preserved for import resolution.
  // 
  // **Warning:** Only ignore packages you do **not** import in your code. Because an ignored package is analyzed as an empty module, any code that imports from it (e.g. `from numpy import array`) or accesses its members will report errors. This setting is intended to reduce analysis cost for large installed packages that your workspace never uses.
  // 
  // **Note:** Only directory (regular or namespace) packages are supported — a single-file module such as `six.py` is not matched and is silently ignored. Namespace packages (e.g. `"google"`) will block all sub-packages. Packages installed as editable installs (`pip install -e`) are not affected. Auto-import suggestions may still include symbols from ignored packages.
  "python.analysis.ignoredPackages": [],

  // Include alias symbols from user files. This will make alias symbols to show up in features such as `add import`, `auto import`
  "python.analysis.includeAliasesFromUserFiles": false,

